cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use these summaries or the tool in this repository, please cite the
  repository. If you are citing a paper I summarised, cite the paper - the
  summary is my reading of it, not a source for its claims.
title: "research-notebook"
abstract: >-
  A public research notebook: paper summaries written in my own words, across
  medical imaging, computer vision, foundation models, multimodal AI, AI
  systems and machine-learning theory, together with the copyright guard that
  checks what may be published here.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Mahmoud
    family-names: Salem
    email: ma7moudalysalem@gmail.com
    # ORCID: DELIBERATELY ABSENT until a real one exists.
    #
    # Registering takes about four minutes at https://orcid.org/register, and it
    # is the identifier that survives changing institution, so it is worth doing
    # this week. But a placeholder ORCID in a citation file is a false claim in
    # the one file whose entire purpose is being trusted by a citation manager,
    # and a fabricated identifier is worse than a missing one.
    #
    # When you have it, uncomment and paste it:
    # orcid: "https://orcid.org/XXXX-XXXX-XXXX-XXXX"
repository-code: "https://github.com/ma7moudalysalem/research-notebook"
url: "https://github.com/ma7moudalysalem/research-notebook"
# Two licences, because this repository is two kinds of thing: the prose is
# CC BY 4.0 and the code is MIT, as COPYRIGHT.md sets out. A bare
# `license: MIT` told a citation manager that the summaries were MIT-licensed,
# which they are not. CFF cannot bind a licence to a path, so the trade is that
# a reader sees both identifiers and has to open license-url to learn which
# applies where.
license:
  - CC-BY-4.0
  - MIT
license-url: "https://github.com/ma7moudalysalem/research-notebook/blob/main/COPYRIGHT.md"
keywords:
  - machine learning
  - medical imaging
  - computer vision
  - foundation models
  - literature review
